  "worked_solution": "The normal is vertical if the tangent is horizontal. Therefore, we require ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ while ${x}^{\u2032}(t)\\ne 0.$\n\nSo, we first find the derivatives of $x$ and $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(t)=7,\\,{y}^{\u2032}(t)=6{t}^{2}-18.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving ${y}^{\u2032}(t)=0$ gives\n\n$$\n6({t}^{2}-3)=0\\,\u27f9\\,t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\n$$\n\nNow, we note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{\u2032}(-\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0, \\\\ {x}^{\u2032}(\\sqrt{3}) & =7\\ne 0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich confirms that the normal to the curve is indeed vertical when $t=\\pm \\sqrt{3}.$",
